 \begin{abstract}
-Bidding agents on web platforms increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of their reasoning tokens goes. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5--7 bids with displayed win probabilities drawn from eBay price data, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and each choice is scored on held-out auctions against an oracle calculator. The models recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Their arithmetic is not the problem: in traces parsed with a validated parser, stated expected profits are almost always right. The loss comes at the end. Traces either run out of tokens before every option is compared, or the final answer ignores the comparison the trace has made. Picking the best option the trace has already evaluated, with the same tokens, would recover 81--100\% of the lead. None of the ex-ante signals we tested predicts which decisions reasoning will improve on a product it was not trained on.
+Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5--7 bids with win probabilities drawn from eBay price data, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and each choice is scored on held-out auctions against an oracle calculator. The models recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Their arithmetic is not the problem: in parsed traces, stated expected profits are almost always right. The loss comes at the end, when traces run out of tokens before every option is compared or the final answer ignores the comparison already made. Picking the best option the trace has already evaluated, with the same tokens, would recover 80--100\% of the lead. A router trained on known products only modestly predicts where reasoning helps, and fails on a new product.
 \end{abstract}
 
