 \section{Introduction}
 
-Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction. Ad exchanges for display advertising moved to first-price, pay-your-bid auctions in 2019 \cite{despotakis2021firstprice}, and LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate for users of online marketplaces \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}. Such agents increasingly run on reasoning models, which spend tokens before acting; a designer can instead give the model a tool for the arithmetic \cite{schick2023toolformer,gao2023pal,chen2023pot}. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost on a bidding decision, and whether an agent can tell in advance when the tokens are worth spending.
+Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction, and platforms increasingly hand the bidder the hard part of the forecast. Ad platforms and marketplaces expose a bid landscape or win-rate simulator, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice of bid to the advertiser, seller or buyer, or to the agent acting for them. Display-ad exchanges have run first-price, pay-your-bid auctions since 2019 \cite{despotakis2021firstprice}, and LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate in such markets \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}. Those agents increasingly run on reasoning models that spend tokens before acting, or they can call a tool for the arithmetic \cite{schick2023toolformer,gao2023pal,chen2023pot}. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost in the bid-landscape decision, and whether an agent can tell in advance when the tokens are worth spending.
 
-The setting is deliberately narrow. Platforms show bidders estimates such as win rates at alternative bids and leave the choice to them. Our agent receives such estimates, so the optimum is known exactly, every lost dollar comes from the agent's own reasoning, and an oracle calculator gives the value of a perfect tool call. We study an internal comparison under a pay-your-bid objective on eBay-derived probabilities, for $\le$14B open checkpoints with at most 768 forced (1{,}024 natural) tokens; we make no claim about bidding on eBay or on ad exchanges, or about tasks in which the agent must estimate the probabilities itself.
+Because the agent receives the estimates, the optimum is known exactly, every lost dollar comes from the agent's own reasoning, and an oracle calculator gives the value of a perfect tool call. The scope follows from that design: one bidder, a pay-your-bid objective, win probabilities estimated from eBay closing prices, and $\le$14B open checkpoints with at most 768 forced (1{,}024 natural) tokens. The question is how well the agent turns a given landscape into a bid, not how it forecasts the landscape or how to bid under eBay's own rules.
 
-We contribute: (1) held-out utility frontiers for six open checkpoints under forced budgets, with uncertainty from value families and the auctions themselves; (2) a validated, additive decomposition of the gap to the calculator into unfinished comparisons and final answers that ignore finished ones, with a same-token counterfactual readout; (3) tests of selective spending scored on both dollar and quality gains, including cost-charged probes, a learned router and an oracle bound. Code, saved decisions and scripts are in an anonymous repository.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/reasoning-compute-economic-utility-EED7/}}
+We contribute: (1) held-out utility frontiers for six open checkpoints under forced budgets, with uncertainty from value families and the auctions themselves; (2) a decomposition of the gap to the calculator into unfinished comparisons and final answers that ignore finished ones, stable across parser tolerances, with a same-token counterfactual readout; (3) a nested, grouped evaluation of selective spending. Code, saved decisions and scripts are in an anonymous repository.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/reasoning-compute-economic-utility-EED7/}}
 
 \section{Related work}
 
-Test-time scaling trades tokens for accuracy through longer chains of thought, sampling and search \cite{wei2022chain,snell2024scaling,wu2024inference,alomrani2025budget}; our forcing resembles budget forcing \cite{muennighoff2025s1}. Work on overthinking \cite{chen2024overthinking}, length control \cite{aggarwal2025l1} and learned think-or-not decisions \cite{zhang2025adaptthink} asks when reasoning is needed; \citet{wan2026shadowprice} allocate tokens across queries for accuracy. We ask per decision, in held-out dollars and quality, and locate the loss inside the trace. Program-aided and tool-using models offload arithmetic \cite{schick2023toolformer,gao2023pal,chen2023pot}. LLM agents have been studied as auction bidders and economic actors \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar,raman2024steer,fish2025econevals,deng2024bargaining}, and \citet{artola2026games} model providers that compete through test-time compute.
