 \section{Task, models and measurement}
 
 \paragraph{Scenarios and estimator.}
-We use 628 eBay auctions of Palm Pilot PDAs, Xbox consoles and Cartier watches \cite{jank2010modeling,onlineauctionsdataset}, split 70/30 within product (seed 20260819). A scenario gives a private value $v$, $K\in\{5,7\}$ candidate bids and, for each bid, the displayed probability $\hat p(b)$, the empirical share of that product's construction-sample closing prices (95--239 per product) at or below $b$. The agent pays its bid if it wins. A chosen bid is scored with the same estimator on 189 held-out auctions (41 Cartier, 103 Palm, 45 Xbox; median binomial standard error of $p$ at a candidate bid 0.047), as expected profit $U(\hat b)=p(\hat b)(v-\hat b)$ and utility ratio $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$. Because eBay prices come from proxy bidding, closing prices are a biased estimate of first-price win rates; this does not affect the internal comparison, since the models and the calculator see the same inputs and are scored the same way. Eighteen values per product and two candidate sets per width give 216 scenarios. The oracle calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. The \emph{gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$.
