 \paragraph{Trace parser.}
-An option counts as \emph{evaluated} when the trace states a number within 2\% of its expected profit $(v-b)\hat p(b)$; a trace is \emph{complete} when all options are evaluated. Two LLM annotators, blind to the parser and told to label by reading, annotated 150 random traces (896 options); a third relabeled 60 of them with perfect agreement ($\kappa=1.0$). Against the annotations the parser has 92\% precision and 97\% recall per option ($\kappa=0.70$) and slightly over-counts completion (72\% versus 65\% of traces). Of the values the annotators found, 97\% are within 2\% of the truth.
